<meta type=article>
  <title | Lists>
  <author | Enscribe>
</meta>

<section | Item registers>

The same list, written four ways — the canonical `<li>` marker, the `<->` and
`<*>` sigils, and the markdown `-` / `*` idiom — lowers to identical output.

<list>
<li> Canonical named marker
<li> Another item
</list>

<list>
<-> Dash sigil marker
<-> Another item
</list>

<list>
<*> Star sigil marker
<*> Another item
</list>

<list>
- Markdown idiom
- Another item
</list>

<section | Multi-paragraph items>

An item runs from its marker to the next one, so it can hold several paragraphs
with no indentation.

<list>
<li> A single-line item.
<li> An item with more to say.

This second paragraph belongs to the same item — it follows the marker until the
next one, no indentation required.

And a third paragraph, still the same item.
<li> Back to a short item.
</list>

<section | Nested lists>

A `<list>` inside an item nests; the outer list resumes at the next marker. A
nested list may itself be ordered.

<list>
<li> Groceries
<list>
<li> Apples
<li> Bread
</list>
<li> Errands
<list ordered>
<li> Post office
<li> Bank
</list>
<li> Done
</list>

<section | Marker schemes, start, and reversed>

`marker=` sets the CSS `list-style-type`; `start` and `reversed` control an
ordered list's numbering.

<list marker=square>
<li> Square bullets,
<li> set with marker=square.
</list>

<list ordered marker=lower-roman>
<li> Lower-roman numbering,
<li> set with marker=lower-roman.
</list>

<list ordered start=5>
<li> This list starts at five,
<li> then six.
</list>

<list ordered reversed>
<li> This list counts
<li> down.
</list>
